---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Treadmills for Marathon Training (2026): Built for Long Runs";
const description =
  "Six treadmills for marathon training and long runs, checked against maker listings and independent reviews: 60-inch decks, motors for hours, decline, cooling.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-1750",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    category: "Best Overall Treadmill for Marathon Training",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D5YKF4SK?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    reviewUrl: "/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$1,999–$2,499",
    specs: "22 x 60 in. belt · 0–12 mph · -3% to 12% incline · 4.25 CHP · 400 lb capacity · AutoBreeze fan",
    pros: [
      "A 4.25 CHP motor rating with a lifetime motor warranty, the combination long runs need",
      "Decline to -3% for practising downhill sections",
      "A 22 x 60-inch belt and a 400 lb capacity on a folding frame",
      "An AutoBreeze fan that adjusts with the intensity of the workout",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Much of the console is built around iFIT, a paid membership — check the current price",
      "Two years on parts and one year of labour is short cover",
      "Top speed is 12 mph, which limits only quite fast runners",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best all-round treadmill for marathon training: a strong motor rating, decline, a wide deck and a fan, at a sensible price.",
    description: "The Commercial 1750 is our pick for most marathoners because it covers every specification that matters for long runs without a premium price. NordicTrack lists a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor rating, 0 to 12 mph, incline from 12% down to -3% decline and a 400 lb user capacity; Best Buy lists the same motor rating, top speed and price, and Costco’s listing agrees on the top speed. The AutoBreeze fan adjusts with workout intensity, and the 16-inch pivoting touchscreen runs iFIT, where a paid membership adds guided runs and automatic speed and incline changes. The warranty is lifetime on the motor, ten years on the frame, two years on parts and one year of labour. NordicTrack was selling it at $1,999.99 against a $2,499.99 list price, and Best Buy at $1,999. If you do not need 14 mph or a bigger screen, there is little reason to spend more.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-7-8-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.8 AT",
    category: "Best Marathon Treadmill Without a Subscription",
    badge: "No Subscription",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon+7.8+AT+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,999–$2,699",
    specs: "22 x 60 in. deck · 0.5–12 mph · 0–15% incline · 375 lb capacity · pace readout · fan",
    pros: [
      "A console that shows pace as well as speed, and no subscription needed for any of it",
      "Lifetime frame and motor warranty, with five years on parts and two years of labour",
      "A 22 x 60-inch three-zone cushioned deck and a 375 lb rating",
      "Bluetooth FTMS for running apps if you want them",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No decline, so downhill courses cannot be practised",
      "Horizon publishes no motor horsepower rating",
      "Horizon’s specification table says it is not compatible with GFCI outlets",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners on flat or rolling courses who want a strong warranty and a clear console, with no membership.",
    description: "The 7.8 AT is the pick for runners who want nothing to do with a subscription. Horizon lists a 22 by 60-inch three-zone cushioned deck, 0.5 to 12 mph, incline from 0 to 15% and a 375 lb maximum user weight; BarBend’s review agrees on each figure, and Garage Gym Reviews and Treadmill Review Guru match on the capacity and top speed. Horizon’s own comparison table lists pace among its workout readouts, which the cheaper 7.0 AT does not, and that matters on a three-hour run. It has a console fan, Bluetooth FTMS for apps, QuickDial speed and incline rollers and folds with hydraulic assistance. The warranty is the strongest among the mid-priced picks: lifetime on the frame and motor, five years on parts and two years of labour. Horizon does not publish a horsepower figure, so we have not printed one. The real limitation for marathon training is that it has no decline. Horizon was selling it at $1,999 against a $2,699 list price when we checked.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-x16",
    name: "NordicTrack X16",
    category: "Best Treadmill for Downhill Courses",
    badge: "Most Decline",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+X16+Incline+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,699–$3,699",
    specs: "22 x 60 in. belt · 0–12 mph · -6% to 40% incline · 4.25 CHP · 400 lb capacity · non-folding",
    pros: [
      "Decline to -6%, twice that of any other machine here",
      "A non-folding frame rated to 400 lb, which reviewers read as a sign of solid build",
      "A 4.25 CHP motor rating with a lifetime motor warranty",
      "Incline to 40% for steep climbing work in the same block",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Large, heavy and does not fold",
      "Most of the console and its automatic adjustments are built around a paid iFIT membership — check the current price",
      "Top speed is 12 mph",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners preparing for a course with long descents, who have the space for a non-folding machine.",
    description: "The X16 earns its place for one reason: it goes downhill further than any other treadmill we could verify. NordicTrack lists incline from -6% decline to 40%, a 22 by 60-inch belt, 0 to 12 mph, a 4.25 CHP motor rating and a 400 lb user capacity; Treadmill Review Guru’s review agrees on each figure and notes the non-folding frame. Six percent of decline lets you rehearse the steeper descents of hilly road courses, and the 40% maximum incline adds steep climbing work that most treadmills cannot offer. It has an AutoBreeze fan and a pivoting touchscreen that runs iFIT. The price of all that range is size: it is a large, heavy machine that does not fold, and the deck sits higher than a normal treadmill’s, so measure your ceiling as well as your floor. NordicTrack was selling it at $2,699.99 against a $3,699.99 list price, with a lifetime motor warranty.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-2450",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
    category: "Best Marathon Treadmill for Speed Work",
    badge: "Most Speed",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D5YKDXDV?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,499–$2,999",
    specs: "22 x 60 in. belt · 0–14 mph · -3% to 12% incline · 4.25 CHP · 400 lb capacity · 24 in. pivoting screen",
    pros: [
      "14 mph leaves room for strides and fast intervals in the same machine",
      "The same 4.25 CHP rating, 22 x 60-inch belt and 400 lb capacity as the 1750",
      "Decline to -3% and an AutoBreeze fan",
      "A 24-inch screen for following routes on long runs, with an iFIT membership",
    ],
    cons: [
      "About $500 more than the 1750 for speed most marathoners will not use",
      "Much of the console is built around iFIT, a paid membership — check the current price",
      "Two years on parts and one year of labour",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For fast marathoners whose speed work goes beyond 12 mph, and for anyone who wants the biggest screen for long runs.",
    description: "The Commercial 2450 is the 1750 with more speed and a bigger screen, and for most marathoners that is not worth the extra money. For fast runners, it is. NordicTrack lists 0 to 14 mph, incline from 12% down to -3% decline, a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor rating, a 400 lb user capacity and an AutoBreeze fan, and Treadmill Review Guru’s review agrees on each figure. That 14 mph is a 4:17-per-mile pace, which matters for strides and short intervals rather than for marathon pace itself. The 24-inch pivoting touchscreen runs iFIT, which with a paid membership plays scenic routes and adjusts speed and incline for you; it is the most immersive screen here for a long run. The warranty is lifetime on the motor, ten years on the frame, two years on parts and one year of labour. NordicTrack was selling it at $2,499.99 against a $2,999.99 list price. If your fastest sessions stay under 12 mph, buy the 1750.",
  },
  {
    id: "wahoo-kickr-run",
    name: "Wahoo KICKR RUN",
    category: "Best Premium Treadmill for Course Simulation",
    badge: "Best Premium",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wahoo+KICKR+RUN+Smart+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $7,000",
    specs: "4:00/mile (15 mph) top speed · -3% to 15% grade · 250 lb capacity · lateral tilt · 1-year warranty",
    pros: [
      "Wahoo says it does not require a subscription to run",
      "With a Wahoo App membership, Route Simulation replays a race course with its grade changes",
      "Decline to -3% and a side-to-side tilt that Wahoo says reproduces off-camber ground",
      "RunFree mode lets the belt follow your pace without pressing anything",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 250 lb maximum user weight is the lowest on this page",
      "One year of warranty cover on a machine that costs about $7,000",
      "Deck dimensions differ between Wahoo and independent coverage, so we have printed neither",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners who want to rehearse a specific course indoors and can justify the price.",
    description: "The KICKR RUN is the treadmill for runners who want to run their race before race day. Wahoo’s Route Simulation, which needs a Wahoo App membership, loads a course file and changes grade and pace as you go, using the machine’s -3% to 15% grade and a side-to-side tilt. Wahoo lists a maximum speed of 4:00 per mile, the -3% to 15% grade and a 250 lb maximum user weight; Treadmill Review Guru gives the same 15 mph, grade range and 250 lb figures. Wahoo lists a 60 by 22-inch running surface while that review lists a longer deck, so we have left the deck size off the card. Wahoo says the treadmill does not require a subscription, though apps may charge their own fees, and that it needs a dedicated circuit and a non-GFCI outlet. It was $6,999.99 on Wahoo’s store when we checked, with a one-year warranty both sources confirm. That warranty, and the 250 lb rating, are the reasons to think hard before spending this much.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-7-0-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.0 AT",
    category: "Best Budget Treadmill for Marathon Training",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon+7.0+AT+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: "/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$1,099–$2,100",
    specs: "20 x 60 in. deck · 0.5–12 mph · 0–15% incline · 325 lb capacity · fan",
    pros: [
      "A full 60-inch deck and 12 mph for about $1,100",
      "Lifetime frame and motor warranty",
      "Garage Gym Reviews’ testers report no shaking during runs",
      "Bluetooth for running apps and Apple Watch support, with no subscription required",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Horizon’s comparison table does not list pace among its readouts, unlike the 7.8 AT",
      "A 20-inch belt asks for a straighter line over a long run than a 22-inch one",
      "No decline, three years on parts and one year of labour",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest treadmill we would trust with a marathon block. Spend more if you want decline, a wider deck or a pace readout.",
    description: "The 7.0 AT is the least expensive treadmill here and the floor for serious long-distance training. Horizon lists a 20 by 60-inch three-zone cushioned deck, 0.5 to 12 mph, incline from 0 to 15% and a 325 lb maximum user weight; Garage Gym Reviews’ 2026 review agrees on each, and its testers report the machine stayed steady during runs, sprints and steady-state sessions. The frame and motor are covered for life, which is the most reassuring single fact for a machine that will be asked to run for hours. It has a fan, Bluetooth for apps, Apple Watch support and hydraulic folding. Three things hold it back for marathon work: a narrower 20-inch belt, no decline, and a console whose readouts, according to Horizon’s own comparison table, include speed but not pace. Horizon was selling it at $1,099 against a $2,100 list price when we checked; reviewers note it usually sells near that figure.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmills for Marathon Training", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-for-marathon-training/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best treadmill for marathon training?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most runners, one with a 60-inch deck, a continuous motor rating with a long motor warranty, a 12 mph top speed and ideally some decline. Of the machines we could verify, the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 fits best, with a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP rating, -3% decline and an automatic fan. The Horizon 7.8 AT is the pick if you want no subscription.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you train for a marathon on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, many runners do much of their training indoors, especially in winter or heat. A treadmill is good for controlled pace, practising fuelling and safe running in the dark. Keeping some long runs on the road helps your legs stay used to the surface and the camber you will race on. Our marathon training guide covers how to combine the two.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a treadmill handle a three-hour run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A well-built running treadmill should, if it is maintained. Long runs load the motor continuously, so look for a continuous motor rating and a long motor warranty, keep the belt lubricated on schedule and keep the motor compartment clean. Check your manual for any limit on continuous use, and stop if you notice a burning smell or the motor cutting out.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need decline on a treadmill for marathon training?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if your race has significant descents. Downhill running loads the legs differently, and decline lets you practise it indoors. Most machines with decline go to -3%, which is enough for gentle descents; the NordicTrack X16 goes to -6%. For a flat course, decline is a nice extra rather than a need, and the money may be better spent on a wider deck or warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size treadmill deck do you need for long runs?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At least 60 inches long, and 22 inches wide if you can afford it. Your stride and position on the belt change as you tire late in a long run, and a short or narrow deck makes you concentrate on staying centred for hours. Taller runners and faster runners benefit most from the extra room. A 55-inch deck is fine for walking and jogging but not for marathon training.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is 12 mph fast enough for marathon training?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For almost everyone, yes. Twelve mph is a five-minute mile, and even a sub-three-hour marathon is run at under 9 mph. The extra speed matters for strides and short intervals, and only quite fast runners reach 12 mph in those. If you do, the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 reaches 14 mph and the Wahoo KICKR RUN a four-minute-mile pace.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-15",
      dateModified: "2026-10-15",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-15",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-15",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best treadmill for marathon training",
      "best treadmill for long runs",
      "best treadmill for distance running",
      "marathon treadmill",
      "treadmill with decline",
      "nordictrack commercial 1750",
      "horizon 7.8 at",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#quick-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-besttreadmillsformarathontraining" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-besttreadmillsformarathontraining)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Treadmills for <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Marathon Training</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A marathon block asks a treadmill to do something most never do: run for two or three hours at a time, several times a month, for months. That changes what matters. Here are six machines whose figures held up when we checked them, and the specifications that decide whether a treadmill copes with long runs or just survives them.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#motor-and-cooling" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Motor and Cooling
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 15, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          treadmills. Each was confirmed on 15 October 2026 as currently on sale, and its
          specifications were checked on that date against the manufacturer's own product page
          (NordicTrack, Horizon Fitness and Wahoo) and at least one independent source — published
          reviews from specialist treadmill review sites and, for the NordicTrack Commercial 1750,
          retailer listings at Best Buy and Costco. A figure appears on a product card only where
          those sources agree; where they disagreed, as they did on the Wahoo KICKR RUN's deck
          dimensions, or where a maker publishes no figure, as Horizon does not for motor
          horsepower, we left it out and say so in the text. None of these are our own
          measurements. Prices are those we saw on manufacturer stores, retailers and review
          coverage on 15 October 2026; they change often and list prices are frequently far above
          selling prices, so confirm the price, the specification and the warranty for the exact
          unit before you order. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on this
          page; it does not change which products we list or what we say about them.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review
          equipment. This is a guide to choosing a treadmill for long-distance training, not a
          training plan, coaching or medical advice. Marathon training is demanding, and long
          treadmill runs add heat and hydration questions of their own; if you have a heart
          condition, an injury, a history of heat illness, are pregnant, or are new to distance
          running, talk to a doctor or a qualified coach before you build up your mileage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 6 Best Treadmills for Marathon Training</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#motor-and-cooling" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Motor and Cooling for Two- and Three-Hour Runs</a>
          <a href="#deck-and-cushioning" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Deck Length, Width and the Cushioning Trade-Off</a>
          <a href="#pace-and-speed" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Speed Headroom and a Console That Shows Pace</a>
          <a href="#decline" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Decline for Downhill-Heavy Courses</a>
          <a href="#fans-and-airflow" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Fans, Airflow and Staying Cool</a>
          <a href="#long-run-setup" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Setting Up for Long Treadmill Runs</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmill reviews are written around a 30- or 45-minute run, and most treadmills are
          designed for one. Marathon training is different. A long run of two to three hours keeps
          the motor working continuously, heats the room and the runner, and gives you plenty of
          time to notice a deck that is a little short or a console that only shows miles per
          hour. This page names six treadmills that suit long-distance training, says plainly what
          each one gives up, and explains the specifications that separate a marathon machine from
          a treadmill that simply has a big number on the box.
        </p>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Six full-size running treadmills, from about $1,100 to about $7,000. The short version:
            the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 for most marathoners, because it combines a 22 by
            60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor rating, decline and an automatic fan; the Horizon 7.8 AT
            if you want no subscription in the picture; and the NordicTrack X16 if your race is
            downhill. If you are planning the training rather than the purchase, start with our
            <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill marathon training guide</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
