              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4:30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10:18</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.8 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">11:27</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.2 mph</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The figures are arithmetic over 26.2 miles, rounded. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            has every conversion. Twelve mph, a five-minute mile, is enough for almost every
            marathoner's speed work. The Commercial 2450's 14 mph and the KICKR RUN's 4:00-per-mile
            top speed matter only to quite fast runners.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pace on the console</h3>
          <p>
            Marathoners think in minutes per mile; most treadmills think in miles per hour. A
            console that shows pace saves you converting in your head for three hours. Check this on
            the exact model, because it varies even within one range: Horizon's own comparison table
            lists pace among the 7.8 AT's workout readouts but not among the 7.0 AT's. NordicTrack's
            touchscreens and the KICKR RUN, which Wahoo describes in pace terms throughout, give you
            more ways to view the data, but confirm the field you want is shown in the mode you
            will actually use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treadmill speed is also not always accurate. If your long runs are paced to the second,
            read our guides to <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">checking treadmill speed</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration</a>,
            or pace with a foot pod you trust outdoors as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Decline for Downhill-Heavy Courses</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmills only go up. For a flat marathon that hardly matters, but some courses
            have long descents, and running downhill uses your legs differently: the muscles work
            while lengthening to brake each stride, and that is the kind of load that leaves legs
            sore late in a downhill race. A treadmill with powered decline lets you practise it
            indoors. Our guide to <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmill decline is good for</a>
            covers the reasons in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four machines here have decline. The NordicTrack Commercial 1750 and 2450 and the
            Wahoo KICKR RUN reach -3%; the NordicTrack X16 reaches -6%. Three percent is a gentle
            but genuine descent, enough to practise downhill running on most courses. Six percent is
            closer to the steepest stretches of the hilliest road races. The two Horizons have no
            decline at all. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a>
            compares every option we could verify.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. Downhill running is harder on the legs than it feels at the time, so
            build it in gradually and in short segments rather than adding long downhill blocks to
            your longest runs straight away. And if you have knee or other joint problems, ask a
            physiotherapist or doctor whether downhill work suits you before you buy a machine for
            it. We review equipment; this is not medical advice.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your race is flat, do not pay for decline you will not use. The money is better spent
            on a wider deck or a better warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fans-and-airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Fans, Airflow and Staying Cool</h2>
<p>
            Heat is the most underestimated problem of long treadmill runs. Outdoors, moving air
            cools you as you run; on a treadmill you are stationary in still air, and a two-hour run
            in a small room turns it warm and humid, which is one reason the same pace can feel
            harder indoors than outside.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every machine here has some answer. The Horizons have console fans. NordicTrack's
            AutoBreeze fan, on all three NordicTracks here, adjusts its speed with the intensity of
            your workout. Wahoo says its separately sold KICKR Headwind fan aligns with the KICKR
            RUN's open console and can follow your heart rate. Be realistic about built-in fans,
            though: they are small and aimed at your chest and face, and on a long run most people
            want more air than they provide.
          </p>
          <p>
            A floor or pedestal fan positioned in front of and slightly to the side of the
            treadmill is the single most useful accessory for marathon training indoors. Add cross-
            ventilation if you can, keep the room cool before you start rather than trying to cool
            it during the run, and keep a drink within reach of the handrail. Our guides to
            <a href="/how-to-stay-cool-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">staying cool on a treadmill</a>
            and the <a href="/best-treadmill-fans/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill fans</a>
            go into detail. If you feel dizzy, confused or stop sweating during a hot session, stop
            running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-run-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Setting Up for Long Treadmill Runs</h2>
<p>
            The machine is only half of a good long run indoors. A few set-up decisions make the
            difference between a session you can repeat every week and one you dread.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Placement.</strong> Put the treadmill where air can move, not in a closed
              box room, and leave the clearance behind it that your manual specifies. Wahoo, for
              example, specifies clearance behind and on each side of the KICKR RUN. Our guide to
              <a href="/where-to-put-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to put a treadmill</a>
              covers the trade-offs.</li>
            <li><strong>A mat.</strong> It protects the floor, keeps dust away from the motor and
              cuts noise for the neighbours over a long session. See the
              <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Fuel and fluids within reach.</strong> Set up the drink and gels you plan to
              race with where you can reach them without stepping off. Practising race-day fuelling
              is one of the genuine advantages of a treadmill long run.</li>
            <li><strong>Something to watch or follow.</strong> Long runs indoors are mentally hard.
              A tablet holder, a route on screen or a structured session helps; our tips on
              <a href="/how-to-make-treadmill-running-less-boring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making treadmill running less boring</a>
              are aimed at exactly this.</li>
            <li><strong>Vary the grade.</strong> Running at a fixed setting for hours is unlike any
              road. Small changes of incline through a long run break the monotony, and a course
              profile does it for you on machines that support one.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety key.</strong> Clip it on for long runs as for any other. Fatigue
              late in a run is when people drift back.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Splitting long runs between road and treadmill, rather than doing them all indoors, keeps
            the legs used to the surface you will race on. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">marathon training guide</a>
            discusses how to combine the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            These are full running treadmills, and not every runner training for a long race needs
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will only do the odd run indoors</strong> when the weather is bad, a
            marathon-grade machine is a lot to spend. A good everyday running treadmill with a
            60-inch deck will cope with occasional long runs; see the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a>
            and the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $2,000</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are training for a half marathon or a first 10K,</strong> the same
            principles apply but the stakes are lower; a shorter long run puts much less sustained
            load on the motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your training is mostly fast intervals,</strong> control layout and top
            speed matter more than motor endurance and decline; our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for HIIT</a>
            is the better starting point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 300 lb,</strong> look closely at capacity. The
            NordicTrack machines here are rated to 400 lb and the Horizon 7.8 AT to 375 lb, but the
            KICKR RUN is rated to only 250 lb. Long runs put more cumulative load on the deck and
            motor than short ones, so a wide margin above your own weight is sensible; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill for marathon training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most runners, one with a 60-inch deck, a continuous motor rating with a long motor warranty, a 12 mph top speed and ideally some decline. Of the machines we could verify, the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 fits best, with a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP rating, -3% decline and an automatic fan. The Horizon 7.8 AT is the pick if you want no subscription.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you train for a marathon on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, many runners do much of their training indoors, especially in winter or heat. A treadmill is good for controlled pace, practising fuelling and safe running in the dark. Keeping some long runs on the road helps your legs stay used to the surface and the camber you will race on. Our marathon training guide covers how to combine the two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill handle a three-hour run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-built running treadmill should, if it is maintained. Long runs load the motor continuously, so look for a continuous motor rating and a long motor warranty, keep the belt lubricated on schedule and keep the motor compartment clean. Check your manual for any limit on continuous use, and stop if you notice a burning smell or the motor cutting out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need decline on a treadmill for marathon training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your race has significant descents. Downhill running loads the legs differently, and decline lets you practise it indoors. Most machines with decline go to -3%, which is enough for gentle descents; the NordicTrack X16 goes to -6%. For a flat course, decline is a nice extra rather than a need, and the money may be better spent on a wider deck or warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size treadmill deck do you need for long runs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 60 inches long, and 22 inches wide if you can afford it. Your stride and position on the belt change as you tire late in a long run, and a short or narrow deck makes you concentrate on staying centred for hours. Taller runners and faster runners benefit most from the extra room. A 55-inch deck is fine for walking and jogging but not for marathon training.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 12 mph fast enough for marathon training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For almost everyone, yes. Twelve mph is a five-minute mile, and even a sub-three-hour marathon is run at under 9 mph. The extra speed matters for strides and short intervals, and only quite fast runners reach 12 mph in those. If you do, the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 reaches 14 mph and the Wahoo KICKR RUN a four-minute-mile pace.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for Marathon Training</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these comes from choosing a treadmill for a 30-minute run and then asking it to do three hours.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the motor by its peak rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A big peak horsepower figure says nothing about how a motor copes with hours of continuous work. Look for a continuous rating and, above all, the length of the motor warranty. A lifetime motor warranty from a maker that has been selling treadmills for years tells you more than any number printed on the hood.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Settling for a short deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 55-inch deck feels fine for a 20-minute test in a shop. Two hours into a long run, when your stride has changed and your attention is wandering, it is a constant problem. For marathon training, treat 60 inches as the minimum and 22 inches of width as worth paying for.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the built-in fan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Console fans are small and aimed at your face. On a long run in a closed room they are rarely enough, and the heat that builds up makes a steady pace feel harder than it should. Budget for a proper floor fan and ventilation alongside the treadmill, and set them up before the first long run.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping maintenance during the block</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The months of a marathon block are when a treadmill does the most work, and when owners are least inclined to spend time on it. A dry belt or a dusty motor compartment makes the motor run hotter on every stride. Lubricate and vacuum on the schedule in your manual, especially before the longest runs.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

